\texttt{identify-life-stages-1} shows cross-model precision matching ($55\%$/$10\%$/$+45$~pp in both models).
\texttt{find-non-living-thing} shows zero deaths in both models, consistent with its majority-class answer distribution (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Cross-model routing transfer is vacuously correct: Llama produces zero internal-preferred episodes, so Qwen-derived routing rules (always-deterministic) trivially match, confirming that routing signal is environment-determined rather than model-specific.
A third model family, Gemma-2-9B-it~\citep{gemma2-2024} (sliding window attention, vs.\ full attention in Qwen/Llama), independently confirms the pattern: DAF${=}89.7\%$ (95\% CI $[68.8\%, 100.0\%]$; $N{=}220$), with all 10 death-prone task types showing positive $\Delta$DR (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}). Three architecturally distinct models thus converge on DAF $88$--$90\%$ (spread 2.2~pp); $10{\times}$ scaling to 72B (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) confirms persistence across capability levels.

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Death\% & $N$ \\
\midrule
Single-trial baseline & 42.9 & 245 \\
Reflexion ($3{\times}$) & 31.4 & 220 \\
Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$) & 30.6 & 255 \\
Deterministic baseline & 16.3 & 245 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\textit{10 death-prone tasks only:}} \\
Death-prone baseline & 48.0 & 200 \\
Retry ($5{\times}$) & 43.5 & 200 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Internal-strategy death rates under agent improvement methods. Top: all 11 tasks (72B: independently collected at both internal and deterministic). Bottom: 10 death-prone tasks (\texttt{find-non-living-thing} excluded, 0\% death at all scales). All interventions reduce death rate but none approaches deterministic-level safety.}
\label{tab:agent-improvement}
\end{table}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry ($N{=}5$ attempts without self-correction) yields marginal reduction on death-prone tasks ($43.5\%$ vs.\ $48.0\%$ baseline, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp). Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} (verbal self-reflection between trials) reduces death to 31.4\% across all 11 tasks by rescuing 29.3\% of first-trial deaths, but the gap to deterministic remains 15.1~pp; on the 10 death-prone tasks, Reflexion achieves ${\sim}$35\%. Under Reflexion ($N{=}3$, matched-episode analysis), $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = -0.47$, yielding DAF $> 100\%$: the deterministic advantage is entirely attributable to death avoidance, and in non-death episodes the self-correcting agent slightly outperforms the deterministic baseline. Scaling to Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$ parameters; both internal and deterministic strategies independently collected at 72B, 255 episodes across all 11 task types) reduces death rate to $30.6\%$. The 72B deterministic strategy achieves $2.75\%$ death rate ($\Delta\text{DR} = +27.8$~pp), and $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.28$, yielding DAF $= 92.4\%$ (7B: $90.4\%$); scaling lowers death rate but leaves death dominance intact.

\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
We extend both interventions to $N{=}5$ trials on death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose-response}). Retry is nearly flat ($48.0\% \to 43.5\%$, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp over five trials). Reflexion drops steeply to $34.0\%$ at $N{=}3$ ($\Delta = -13.5$~pp, $z = 2.75$, $p = 0.003$) then plateaus ($34$--$36.5\%$ for $N \geq 3$, pairwise $p > 0.5$). With $n{=}200$ paired episodes, the minimum detectable effect is ${\sim}8$~pp ($\alpha = 0.05$, 80\% power): the key $N{=}1{\to}3$ decline is well-powered; the plateau claim ($N{=}3{\to}5$, $+1.0$~pp) is also supported, since a ${\geq}8$~pp improvement would be detected. We characterize this as suggestive evidence of a Reflexion ceiling rather than a conclusive demonstration. Retry's per-trial conditional death rate stays constant at ${\sim}48\%$; Reflexion's declines to ${\sim}31\%$ by Trial~3 (per-task breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:dose-response}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Death rate as a function of trial count ($N$) for Retry and Reflexion on 10 death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$). Retry (blue) is nearly flat; Reflexion (red) drops steeply through $N{=}3$ then plateaus. Horizontal lines: 72B scaling baseline ($30.6\%$, all 11 tasks) and deterministic baseline ($16.3\%$). Error bands: Wilson 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:dose-response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Environment Boundary Probes}
\label{sec:math-contrast}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We examine MATH and ALFWorld as boundary probes---not cross-domain validation---testing framework applicability across environments that instantiate ``deterministic computation'' differently (oracle simulator, code interpreter, admissible-action enumeration).

\paragraph{MATH.} On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$, code interpreter), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy ($73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$, McNemar $p = 0.81$), with 74.6\% no-preference. Without catastrophic failure, no routing signal emerges.

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} (6 task types, 135 games) provides the deterministic strategy with admissible actions---an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch, with no death mechanism ($\delta = 0$, DAF $= 0\%$). With Qwen2.5-7B, 35 of 37 non-tie labels (94.6\%) favor the information-advantaged strategy (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env-details}); Llama-3.1-8B (135 games) yields 16 of 16 non-tie labels in the same direction (100\%). Llama shows fewer non-tie episodes (11.9\% vs.\ 25.9\%) but perfect directional consistency, confirming that information asymmetry---not death avoidance---drives routing signal in ALFWorld regardless of model family. The framework diagnoses this qualitatively different mechanism (DAF $= 0\%$ vs.\ $90.4\%$) without modification.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 20--40 episodes per type, 245 total). $\dagger$\,Single random seed (42) per episode pair; see Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} for 8-seed robustness analysis.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic---an \emph{oracle degeneracy} where an always-deterministic lookup achieves 90.5\% of non-tie oracle decisions (balanced accuracy 50.0\%: perfect deterministic recall, zero internal recall).

\subsection{Router Training: A Negative Result}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside. The near-chance step-level accuracy admits two interpretations: (A)~episode-level labels projected onto steps create ${\sim}$88\% uninformative signal (60.3\% pre-event neutral, 27.4\% post-event), drowning learnable patterns in noise; (B)~routing signal is genuinely task-type-level---there is no step-level pattern to learn. Directional conflict is only 0.3\% of 2{,}040 training steps, but the granularity mismatch leaves minimal signal. The data scaling ablation (25\%--800\%, no improvement) and extreme class imbalance (86/95 $= 90.5\%$ deterministic-preferred) together favor Interpretation~B (non-learnability): routing signal is genuinely task-type-level, not step-level learnable---though we cannot definitively rule out Interpretation~A.

An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at chance. Episode-level independent evaluation yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below the majority-vote baseline of 50.0\%; this independent set includes held-out task types, so the below-chance result reflects cross-task transfer failure rather than overall router incompetence. Training uses episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode leakage. The high uninformative rate connects to noisy-label learning~\citep{natarajan2013learning}: standard ERM on labels with ${\sim}$88\% noise yields near-chance classifiers, consistent with our results.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}, spanning several functional categories.
\emph{Self-supervised} approaches learn tool invocation from loss signals: Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2023} inserts API calls filtered by perplexity reduction; ToolACE \cite{toolace-2025} curates synthetic data for function-calling accuracy.
\emph{Token-level} methods embed tools into the model vocabulary: ToolkenGPT \cite{toolkengpt-2023} represents tools as special tokens resolved at inference.
\emph{Task-level} decomposition routes subtasks to specialized modules: ART \cite{art-2023} generates tool-augmented programs; Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2023} composes heterogeneous modules per subtask.
\emph{RL-based} methods learn routing policies from reward: ReTool \cite{retool-2025} discovers when code execution is preferable to chain-of-thought.
All focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Concurrent work examines \emph{when} to invoke tools: tool-call decisions correlate with uncertainty~\citep{tool-overuse-illusion-2026}, are encoded in hidden states~\citep{when2tool-2026}, impose a ``tool-use tax''~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, or follow necessity-utility-affordability logic~\citep{to-call-or-not-2026}. Our decomposition provides environment-grounded evidence: 78.9\% of routing labels trace to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
Adaptive routing traces back to learned halting policies \citep{graves2016act}. In the LLM era, CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} adapts reasoning depth per step, ARES \cite{ares-2026} selects among reasoning effort levels, Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths, and MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn routing.
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models.
\citet{budget-aware-routing-2026} train episode-level routing policies under sparse terminal rewards---the closest setting to ours; they optimize \emph{which} model to invoke, whereas we diagnose \emph{what} drives the routing decision.

\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation in RL.}
Prior work generates counterfactual explanations for RL agents by perturbing individual actions \citep{olson2021counterfactual, gajcin2023counterfactual, amitai2024explaining}; recent extensions target LLM agents via action-level abstraction~\citep{pona2025abstract}, simulator queries~\citep{axis-2026}, and critical-step identification~\citep{cso-2026}.
We instead execute complete episodes under two strategies to decompose routing signal \emph{before} training.

\paragraph{LLM Agent Environments and Reasoning.}
ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022} serves as our primary testbed; ALFWorld \cite{alfworld-2021} provides a complementary household environment; AgentBench \cite{agentbench-2024} offers systematic evaluation. \citet{zhou2024webarena} document common failure modes (premature termination, hallucinated actions) in web-based agents, consistent with our findings. ReAct \cite{react-2023} serves as our internal baseline; Reflexion \cite{reflexion-2023} extends this with verbal self-reflection; LATS \cite{lats-2024} adds tree search; SALT~\citep{salt-2026} assigns step-level credit via trajectory graphs on ALFWorld, complementing our episode-level decomposition.

\paragraph{Safe RL.}
Death avoidance connects to safe RL: constrained MDPs \citep{altman1999constrained} and risk-sensitive RL \citep{mihatsch2002risk} formalize safety through state constraints or CVaR objectives; classical methods \citep{garcia2015comprehensive,tamar2015cvar,achiam2017cpo,crpo-2021,moldovan2012safe} require explicit constraint specification. Our routing achieves safety implicitly through computation mode selection; we diagnose when risk-sensitivity emerges rather than prescribing it. \citet{ruan2024failures} identify catastrophic actions as a distinct failure category, consistent with our finding; SafeRoute \citep{saferoute-2025} applies adaptive model selection for safety guardrails.

